From: Codex <codex@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: hit-test the release of a caption right click with WM_NCHITTEST

Purpose: open the taskbar menu when the unlocked XP taskbar is right
clicked between its bands. That taskbar has no WS_CAPTION; its own
WM_NCHITTEST returns HTCAPTION there so it can be dragged. DefWindowProc
entered the WM_NCRBUTTONDOWN capture loop for HTCAPTION, but tested the
button-up point with Wine's internal default hit test, which only knows
WS_CAPTION windows. It returned HTCLIENT, so WM_CONTEXTMENU was never
sent and nothing appeared. Windows sends WM_CONTEXTMENU for the right
button release in the caption (documented for WM_NCRBUTTONUP); ask the
window with WM_NCHITTEST, as the button-down test did.
tests/nc-context-menu.c covers a captionless popup that returns HTCAPTION.
No Microsoft binary is changed.
Reference: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/menurc/wm-contextmenu

diff --git a/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c b/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
index 84205fa..d0775ba 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
@@ -2294,7 +2294,10 @@ static LRESULT handle_nc_rbutton_down( HWND hwnd, WPARAM wparam, LPARAM lparam )
             if (NtUserCallMsgFilter( &msg, MSGF_MAX )) continue;
             if (msg.message == WM_RBUTTONUP)
             {
-                hittest = handle_nc_hit_test( hwnd, msg.pt );
+                /* Ask the window, as the button-down hit test did. A window
+                 * without WS_CAPTION may return HTCAPTION from its own
+                 * WM_NCHITTEST, as the unlocked XP taskbar does. */
+                hittest = send_message( hwnd, WM_NCHITTEST, 0, MAKELPARAM( msg.pt.x, msg.pt.y ));
                 break;
             }
         }
